{"status":"answered","answer":"The acid-and-leaf experiment is designed to model how the stomach is protected from its own acid. In this experiment, two similar green leaves are used. One leaf is coated with vaseline or petroleum jelly, while the other is left untreated. A few drops of a weak acid are added to both leaves. After some time, observations are made to see the effects of the acid on the leaves.

The leaf without the protective coating is affected by the acid, showing signs of damage. In contrast, the leaf with the vaseline remains unaffected. This demonstrates how the vaseline acts as a protective barrier against the acid.

This experiment is analogous to how the stomach protects itself. The stomach secretes strong acids for digestion, but it is protected by a mucus lining. This mucus acts like the vaseline, forming a barrier that prevents the acid from damaging the stomach walls. Thus, the mucus lining is crucial in protecting the stomach from its own acidic secretions.","selected_evidence_ids":["E1"],"citations":[{"evidence_id":"E1","pdf_page":175,"textbook_page":166}],"missing_information":[]}
